# Makes package.json's "engines": { "node": "24" } binding instead of
# advisory: npm's own documentation for this flag is "npm will
# stubbornly refuse to install (or even consider installing) any
# package that claims to not be compatible with the current Node.js
# version" (npm 11's docs/content/using-npm/config.md). Verified
# empirically, since the wording says "any package" and the one that
# matters here is this repo itself: with this set, both `npm install`
# and `npm ci` exit 1 with EBADENGINE on the wrong Node - and `npm ci`
# reads that engines range from package.json, not from
# package-lock.json's root entry, so the refusal holds even on a lock
# written before the range existed. Without it, the same mismatch is
# only an `npm warn EBADENGINE` and the install proceeds.
#
# Why that matters: every other Node pin in this project (.node-version,
# .inspector.json's cimg/node:24.18.0, the worker/relay images'
# digest-pinned node:24.18.0-alpine, docker-tests.yml via
# node-version-file) describes Node 24, and a suite that quietly runs on
# a different major is how a Node-version-specific failure hides. See
# notes/20261017-make-the-repo-declare-its-own-node-versi-sp.md.
#
# npm never packs .npmrc into a tarball, so this binds work done in a
# checkout of this repo and never leaks into a consumer's install.
engine-strict=true
